Having seen a lizard corpse and not carrying one in your main inventory
would be the rule you broke, if you ask me at least -- being safe can't
hurt, and invisible trices, etc. are another reason.

Being burdened is a completely separate (but worse, in my opinion)
mistake; there are very few excuses for being burdened for any length
of time (these include moving stashes -- under carefully controlled
conditions of course -- as well as sacfesting and such).  Burdened-ness
can lead to massive numbers of YAADs that are not your fault, until you
realize that you were ripping yourself off for turns by carrying that
extra whateveritis and it would have been an easy situation to win /
escape from otherwise.

Going near a cockatrice is not smart unless you have a lizard corpse
ready; deliberately doing it was a bad idea, especially if you had a
lizard corpse and just didn't feel like readying it.

> Stupid.

Don't feel bad, (almost) everybody makes (almost) every mistake once.
You can righteously feel stupid once you've died 2 or 3 times by
voluntary cockatrice meleeing without adequate preparation, but with
luck you've already learned your lesson.  A biggie you should take away
from this, though, is to never consider burdened-ness a way of life.
In your case, you should almost certainly have ditched the massively
heavy plate mail, and either gone with light stuff and been careful or
gone with dwarvish mithril and not cast anything for awhile.  In
battle, would you rather have the better AC or the ability to attack /
make getaways at a halfway decent speed?

